K8 PASS f = [0.4546, 0.69722, 0.83479, 0.9052, 0.942]; all < 1: True   strictly increasing, f<1
      a monotone f is what a single growing mode gives; a non-monotone f would mean the integration or the response sign is wrong
K9 PASS D(1) = 0.740853856 (DOP853) vs 0.740853856 (LSODA) -> ratio-1 = +3.23e-11   |ratio-1| < 1e-6
      two independent adaptive integrators on the same ODE; a sign or friction error would show up here at O(1)

====================================================================================================
5.  THE FORK -- COLD SECTOR IN THE SOURCE, OR ALREADY IN THE RESPONSE?
====================================================================================================
  branch S: sigma8(0) = 0.7619  D(1) = 0.7409  f(0) = 0.4546  f(2) = 0.9420
  branch A: sigma8(0) = 0.0024  D(1) = 0.0023  f(0) = 0.1312  f(2) = 0.2784
  branch C: sigma8(0) = 0.7605  D(1) = 0.7395  f(0) = 0.4526  f(2) = 0.9414
K10 PASS sigma8(S) = 0.7619 vs sigma8(A) = 0.0024 -> ratio 322.847   ratio > 1.5
      putting the cold sector in the source raises sigma8 by tens of per cent; the two readings cannot both be called 'the theory'
K11 PASS M_c/M_b = 5.3642; the law's phantom M_ph/M_b = 25.90; M_c/M_ph = 0.207; g_N,tot/g_N,b = 6.364 -> deep-MOND g ratio sqrt = 2.523   M_src = M_b+M_c and M_ph != M_c
      the action's first integral fixes M_src = M_b + M_c: the law relates the total field to the total Newtonian field. NOT double counting -- the law contains only the SUM. NOTE the cost: evaluated with M_src = 6.3643 M_b the law predicts g = sqrt(6.3643) x the baryon-only deep-MOND value, i.e. 2.523x the SPARC fit's g. Branch S is the action's reading and it is INCONSISTENT with the baryon-only SPARC fits of THEORY.md section 4. Branch C is what those fits use and it needs the cold to be unboosted
  branch R (rms field, amplitude closure): iterations 8, sigma8(0) -> 2.267; g_rms(8/h Mpc, z=0) = 2.241e-13 m/s^2 = 0.0020 a0 -> G_eff/G = 11.75
K12 PASS sigma8(0) fixed point = 2.267 (Planck 0.811; growth-ratio band 0.75-0.85); G_eff/G(g_rms) = 11.7   sigma8 > 1.5 (1.8x the top of the observed band)
      with g_ref = the perturbation's own rms field, G_eff/G = 10-12 at z=0 and the growth is quasi-linear (G_eff depends on delta). This is the SAME failure L341/FP22 recorded for the chassis (sigma8 = 18-27); it is not cured by this background
K13 PASS G_eff(k=0.05)/G_eff(k=0.2) = 0.630 (8.67 vs 13.75); n_eff = -2.4 assumed   ratio differs from 1 by > 20%
      g_N ~ delta R with delta ~ R^{-(3+n)/2}, so g_N grows with R and the boost is LARGER on SMALLER scales: the amplitude closure predicts scale-DEPENDENT growth (a bluer P(k), more small-scale power). 'Scale-free' holds only at fixed g_N, i.e. only for a reference field that is a function of time alone

