7.  SENSITIVITIES -- a0 footing, and the background normalisation
====================================================================================================
  theory_1.132e-10: sigma8(0) = 0.7619, f(0) = 0.4546, G_eff(0)/G = 1.01397, chi2_fs8 = 14.21
  canonical_9.36e-11: sigma8(0) = 0.7615, f(0) = 0.4540, G_eff(0)/G = 1.01025, chi2_fs8 = 14.41
  flat normalisation (Omega_L = 1-Omega_m): sigma8(0) = 0.8132, f(0) = 0.5317  [= LCDM by construction]
  trace-average response (2nu+dg/dgN)/3: sigma8(0) = 0.7836, f(0) = 0.4709, G_eff(0)/G = 1.0933
  literal_OL1_H0dS     Omega_L=1.0000 Omega_K=+0.0000  H(0)= 77.33 km/s/Mpc  D(1)=0.7409  f(0)=0.4546  sigma8(0)=0.7619
  flat_OL1mOm          Omega_L=0.6837 Omega_K=+0.0000  H(0)= 67.40 km/s/Mpc  D(1)=0.7907  f(0)=0.5317  sigma8(0)=0.8132
  closed_OL1_OKmOm     Omega_L=1.0000 Omega_K=-0.3163  H(0)= 67.40 km/s/Mpc  D(1)=1.1371  f(0)=0.5441  sigma8(0)=1.1695
K16 PASS sigma8(0) = 0.7619 (literal) / 0.8132 (flat) / 1.1695 (closed); observed 0.811 +- 0.006 (Planck) and 0.76-0.83 (lensing)   closed > 1.0 (excluded); flat == 0.811
      Omega_L,eff = 1, Omega_m = 0.3163 and H(0) = H0 are three statements that cannot all hold with zero curvature. Resolving them by curvature (closed) gives sigma8 = 1.17, excluded by Planck at tens of sigma; resolving them by H(0) = 1.1473 H0 gives 0.760; cancelling the excess with the beta-stress gives 0.811 = LCDM exactly. The growth observable therefore does not test the law -- it tests which background the theory means

